1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,230
Section 3

2
00:00:02,230 --> 00:00:03,512
STEPHANIE:

3
00:00:03,512 --> 00:00:04,000
Hello, Trevor.

4
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:04,924
TREVOR:

5
00:00:04,924 --> 00:00:05,648
Hello, Stephanie.

6
00:00:05,648 --> 00:00:10,148
You said you wanted to talk about the course I'm taking on literature for

7
00:00:10,148 --> 00:00:10,510
children.

8
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:10,560
STEPHANIE:

9
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:11,566
That's right.

10
00:00:11,566 --> 00:00:14,030
I'm thinking of doing it next year,

11
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:17,550
but I'd like to find out more about it first.

12
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:17,911
TREVOR:

13
00:00:17,911 --> 00:00:21,526
OK, well, as you probably know, it's a one-year course.

14
00:00:21,526 --> 00:00:23,333
It's divided into six modules,

15
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:26,225
and you have to take all of them.

16
00:00:26,225 --> 00:00:31,286
One of the most interesting ones, for me, at least, was about the purpose

17
00:00:31,286 --> 00:00:32,370
of children's literature.

18
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:32,829
STEPHANIE:

19
00:00:32,829 --> 00:00:39,250
You mean, whether it should just entertain children or should be educational as well.

20
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:39,636
TREVOR:

21
00:00:39,636 --> 00:00:40,023
Right,

22
00:00:40,023 --> 00:00:45,044
and whether the teaching should be factual - giving them information about the world -

23
00:00:45,044 --> 00:00:46,975
or ethical, teaching them values.

24
00:00:46,975 --> 00:00:52,383
What's fascinating is that the writer isn't necessarily conscious of the message they're conveying.

25
00:00:52,383 --> 00:00:57,791
For instance, a story might show a child who has a problem as a

26
00:00:57,791 --> 00:01:03,199
result of not doing what an adult has told them to do, implying that

27
00:01:03,199 --> 00:01:05,130
children should always obey adults.

28
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:05,608
STEPHANIE:

29
00:01:05,608 --> 00:01:08,000
I see what you mean.

30
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:08,366
TREVOR:

31
00:01:08,366 --> 00:01:13,119
That module made me realise how important stories are - they can have a

32
00:01:13,119 --> 00:01:16,045
significant effect on children as they grow up.

33
00:01:16,045 --> 00:01:21,164
Actually, it inspired me to have a go at it myself, just for my

34
00:01:21,164 --> 00:01:21,896
own interest.

35
00:01:21,896 --> 00:01:27,015
I know I can't compete with the really popular stories, like the Harry Potter

36
00:01:27,015 --> 00:01:28,478
books - they're very good,

37
00:01:28,478 --> 00:01:32,500
and even young kids like my seven-year-old niece love reading them.

38
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:33,033
STEPHANIE:

39
00:01:33,033 --> 00:01:33,567
Mm.

40
00:01:33,567 --> 00:01:37,300
I'm very interested in illustrations in stories.

41
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:40,500
Is that covered in the course?

42
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:40,960
TREVOR:

43
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:43,720
Yes, there's a module on pictures,

44
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:47,400
and how they're sometimes central to the story.

45
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:47,795
STEPHANIE:

46
00:01:47,795 --> 00:01:48,585
That's good.

47
00:01:48,585 --> 00:01:52,536
I remember some frightening ones I saw as a child

48
00:01:52,536 --> 00:01:57,277
and I can still see them vividly in my mind, years later!

49
00:01:57,277 --> 00:02:01,228
Pictures can be so powerful, just as powerful as words.

50
00:02:01,228 --> 00:02:02,808
I've always enjoyed drawing,

51
00:02:02,808 --> 00:02:08,339
so that the field I want to go into when I finished the course.

52
00:02:08,339 --> 00:02:11,500
I bet that module will be really helpful.

53
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:11,875
TREVOR:

54
00:02:11,875 --> 00:02:13,375
I'm sure it will.

55
00:02:13,375 --> 00:02:16,000
We also studied comics in that module,

56
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,125
but I'm not convinced of their value, not compared with books.

57
00:02:20,125 --> 00:02:25,000
One of the great things about words is that you use your imagination,

58
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
but with a comic you don't have to.

59
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:28,401
STEPHANIE:

60
00:02:28,401 --> 00:02:33,217
But children are so used to visual input - on TV, video games,

61
00:02:33,217 --> 00:02:34,421
and so on.

62
00:02:34,421 --> 00:02:39,637
There are plenty of kids who wouldn't even try to read a book,

63
00:02:39,637 --> 00:02:43,650
so I think comics can serve a really useful purpose.

64
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:44,019
TREVOR:

65
00:02:44,019 --> 00:02:49,190
You mean, it's better to read a comic than not to read at all?

66
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:51,037
Yes, I suppose you're right.

67
00:02:51,037 --> 00:02:54,730
I just think it's sad when children don't read books.

68
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:55,164
STEPHANIE:

69
00:02:55,164 --> 00:02:59,074
What about books for girls and books for boys?

70
00:02:59,074 --> 00:03:01,680
Does the course go into that?

71
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:02,053
TREVOR:

72
00:03:02,053 --> 00:03:04,291
Yes, there's a module on it.

73
00:03:04,291 --> 00:03:09,514
For years, lots of stories, in English, at least, assumed that boys went out

74
00:03:09,514 --> 00:03:14,364
and did adventurous things and girls stayed at home and played with dolls.

75
00:03:14,364 --> 00:03:18,841
I was amazed how many books were targeted at just one sex

76
00:03:18,841 --> 00:03:19,960
or the other.

77
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:24,810
Of course this reflects society as it is when the books are written.

78
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:25,216
STEPHANIE:

79
00:03:25,216 --> 00:03:26,029
That's true.

80
00:03:26,029 --> 00:03:30,500
So it sounds as though you think it's a good course.

81
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:31,470
TREVOR:

82
00:03:31,470 --> 00:03:32,440
Definitely.

83
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:32,758
TREVOR:

84
00:03:32,758 --> 00:03:37,215
Have you been reading lots of children's stories, to help you decide whether to

85
00:03:37,215 --> 00:03:38,170
take the course?

86
00:03:38,170 --> 00:03:38,620
STEPHANIE:

87
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:39,070
Yeah.

88
00:03:39,070 --> 00:03:45,370
I've gone as far back as the late seventeenth century, though I know there

89
00:03:45,370 --> 00:03:47,170
were earlier children's stories.

90
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:47,511
TREVOR:

91
00:03:47,511 --> 00:03:50,916
So does that mean you've read Perrault' s fairy tales?

92
00:03:50,916 --> 00:03:52,278
Cinderella, The Sleeping Beauty,

93
00:03:52,278 --> 00:03:53,300
and so on.

94
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:53,740
STEPHANIE:

95
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:54,181
Yes.

96
00:03:54,181 --> 00:03:55,942
They must be important,

97
00:03:55,942 --> 00:04:02,108
because no stories of that type had been written before, these were the first.

98
00:04:02,108 --> 00:04:04,750
Then there's The Swiss Family Robinson.

99
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:05,200
TREVOR:

100
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:07,000
I haven't read that.

101
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:07,425
STEPHANIE:

102
00:04:07,425 --> 00:04:12,952
The English name makes it sound as though Robinson is the family's surname

103
00:04:12,952 --> 00:04:17,203
but a more accurate translation would be the Swiss Robinsons,

104
00:04:17,203 --> 00:04:23,154
because it's about a Swiss family who are shipwrecked, like Robinson Crusoe in the

105
00:04:23,154 --> 00:04:25,280
novel of a century earlier.

106
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:25,742
TREVOR:

107
00:04:25,742 --> 00:04:28,050
Well I never knew that!

108
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:28,455
STEPHANIE:

109
00:04:28,455 --> 00:04:32,500
Have you read Hoffmann's The Nutcracker and the Mouse King?

110
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:32,950
TREVOR:

111
00:04:32,950 --> 00:04:37,000
Wasn't that the basis for Tchaikovsky's ballet The Nutcracker?

112
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:37,667
STEPHANIE:

113
00:04:37,667 --> 00:04:39,000
That's right.

114
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
It has some quite bizarre elements.

115
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:43,395
TREVOR:

116
00:04:43,395 --> 00:04:46,947
I hope you've read Oscar Wilde's The Happy Prince.

117
00:04:46,947 --> 00:04:50,500
It's probably my favourite children's story of all time.

118
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:50,961
STEPHANIE:

119
00:04:50,961 --> 00:04:51,884
Mine too!

120
00:04:51,884 --> 00:04:53,730
And it's so surprising,

121
00:04:53,730 --> 00:04:57,422
because Wilde is best known for his plays,

122
00:04:57,422 --> 00:05:00,653
and most of them are very witty,

123
00:05:00,653 --> 00:05:03,883
but The Happy Prince is really moving.

124
00:05:03,883 --> 00:05:09,421
I struggled with Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings - three long books,

125
00:05:09,421 --> 00:05:12,190
and I gave up after one.

126
00:05:12,190 --> 00:05:12,752
TREVOR:

127
00:05:12,752 --> 00:05:15,000
It's extremely popular, though.

128
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:15,333
STEPHANIE:

129
00:05:15,333 --> 00:05:15,667
Yeah,

130
00:05:15,667 --> 00:05:20,000
but whereas something like The Happy Prince just carried me along with it.

131
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,667
The Lord of the Rings took more effort than I was prepared to give

132
00:05:24,667 --> 00:05:25,000
it.

133
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:25,416
TREVOR:

134
00:05:25,416 --> 00:05:28,330
I didn't find that - I love it.

135
00:05:28,330 --> 00:05:28,766
STEPHANIE:

136
00:05:28,766 --> 00:05:31,820
Another one I've read is War Horse.

137
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:32,215
TREVOR:

138
00:05:32,215 --> 00:05:33,004
Oh yes.

139
00:05:33,004 --> 00:05:36,162
It's about the First World War, isn't it?

140
00:05:36,162 --> 00:05:39,320
Hardly what you'd expect for a children's story.

141
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:39,874
STEPHANIE:

142
00:05:39,874 --> 00:05:40,427
Exactly,

143
00:05:40,427 --> 00:05:43,195
but it's been very successful.

144
00:05:43,195 --> 00:05:45,410
Have you read any...
